          .num_cor
            %h6 Corollary
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/table+of+marks"} table of marks
              of a finite group determines its
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Burnside+ring"} Burnside ring
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi G
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_{1}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi G
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_{2}
              are
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equal
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi G
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_{1}
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphic
              to the Burnside ring of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi G
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_{2}
          %p
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              The Burnside ring of a finite group is a free abelian group on the set
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %msub
                        %mi H
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi …
                      %mo ,
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %msub
                        %mi H
                        %mi t
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G / H_1, \ldots, G / H_t
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi H
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi H
                      %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_1, \ldots, H_t
              are representatives of the conjugacy classes of that group, equipped with a certain multiplication. Thus it suffices to check that the structure constants of the Burnside rings coincide, which is established by the previous proposition.
          %h2#applications Applications
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Burnside+ring+is+equivariant+stable+cohomotopy+of+the+point"} Burnside ring is equivariant stable cohomotopy of the point
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/character+table"} character table
          %h2#references References
          %p The concept was introduced in
          %ul
            %li#Burnside1897
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Burnside"}> William Burnside
                , chapter XII of
